WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.640
Your blood must grab oxygen in the lungs and let it go in working muscle.

2
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:09.900
How can one protein do both?

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.740
In the lungs, the oxygen pressure is about 100 torr.

4
00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.500
Oxygen is plentiful there.

5
00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:20.503
Blood leaving resting tissue sits near 40 torr.

6
00:00:21.483 --> 00:00:23.703
Blood leaving hard-working muscle sits near 20.

7
00:00:24.783 --> 00:00:26.663
So blood has two opposite jobs.

8
00:00:27.543 --> 00:00:29.123
Load oxygen where it is plentiful.

9
00:00:30.143 --> 00:00:31.203
Unload it where it is scarce.

10
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:35.283
A protein that binds tightly is good at the first job.

11
00:00:36.103 --> 00:00:37.143
It is bad at the second.

12
00:00:38.183 --> 00:00:43.503
Predict. Would a protein that binds oxygen more tightly make a better carrier?

13
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:55.353
No. A tight binder fills in the lungs, but it stays nearly full in the muscle.

14
00:00:56.373 --> 00:00:58.553
Oxygen that stays aboard is not delivered.

15
00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:03.877
Myoglobin, the muscle's storage protein, is that kind of tight binder.

16
00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:08.057
Across the same gap it would unload only about seven percent.

17
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.640
Hemoglobin gives up about two-thirds of its oxygen between the lungs and

18
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:14.640
hard-working muscle.

19
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.480
At resting tissue it gives up much less, about a fifth.

20
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.920
Blood leaving resting tissue is still mostly full.

21
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.520
Hard work drains far more.

22
00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.660
The harder the muscle works, the more oxygen the blood hands over.

23
00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.690
Both proteins use the same heme and the same iron.

24
00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.270
So what is hemoglobin doing differently?

25
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.460
Myoglobin has one binding site.

26
00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:43.540
Hemoglobin has four that cooperate, and its grip is set for the pressures between

27
00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:44.540
lung and muscle.

28
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.570
Here is the idea for this whole series.

29
00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.410
Hemoglobin is always a mix of two shapes.

30
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:54.520
One shape, called T, holds oxygen weakly.

31
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.000
The other, called R, holds it tightly.

32
00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:02.743
How full hemoglobin is depends on how full each shape would be, and how much of each

33
00:02:02.743 --> 00:02:03.743
is present.

34
00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:10.007
Anything that binds one shape better shifts the mix toward that shape.

35
00:02:10.967 --> 00:02:12.287
Oxygen itself favors R.

36
00:02:17.317 --> 00:02:21.677
So oxygen pushes hemoglobin toward the shape that grabs oxygen best.

37
00:02:22.457 --> 00:02:25.177
That is cooperativity, and it is why blood delivers.
